nt on tourism as we know  and people have lost their livelihoods  even left the sector all together but  slowly the situation is improving and  countries are opening up  such as in thailand with the sandbox  project in phuket a very interesting  development happening here which is a  development where we're isolating  tourists again in their tourist bubbles  in resorts  a kind of mass tourism that has been  criticized in the past  and here in this region we're also  seeing digital nomads being targeted  quite strongly to stay for a few months  or even years and  we have to consider these are very  diffic different tourists  um  there might not be responsible ones yeah  in what way are they different  uh they're different because they  obviously have different needs they're  staying much longer they're working  they're not the traditional tourists  we're seeing and they're definitely very  different from other tourists that are  being targeted like domestic tourists or  the high quality tourists that  thailand is trying to target rather than  the backpacker that actually gives more  money to local communities and the rural  countryside and the home stays yeah  i think we really have to think about  sustainability here and we have to also  be realistic that southeast asia is a  region that can't just target the  wealthy traveler but also must target  those uh backpackers they have done  before  right so so  when we take this uh lesson learned if  you like back to europe irena what  lessons can we learn from this pandemic  both for for the tourists and for the  people whose livelihood depends on  tourism what kind of tourism will be  sustainable  tourism that is more localized more  regenerated that connects to local  community to the nature that regenerates  places and resources on the basis of  which tourism actually depends and this  is the trend that we see now for more  conscious travelers who um experiencing  the isolation of pandemic realize how  much they miss 